# Email Configuration for Admin User Creation
# Add these to your .env file and Vercel environment variables

# ============================================
# SMTP Configuration (Required for Email)
# ============================================

# SMTP Host (your email provider's SMTP server)
# Examples:
# - Gmail: smtp.gmail.com
# - Outlook: smtp-mail.outlook.com
# - SendGrid: smtp.sendgrid.net
# - Mailgun: smtp.mailgun.org
# - AWS SES: email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com

# SMTP Port
# - Use 587 for TLS (recommended)
# - Use 465 for SSL
# - Use 25 for non-secure (not recommended)
SMTP_PORT=587

# SMTP Username (usually your email address)
SMTP_USER=your-email@gmail.com

# SMTP Password or App Password
# For Gmail: Generate an App Password at https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
# For other providers: Use your regular password or API key
SMTP_PASS=your-app-password-here

# From Address (sender email)
SMTP_FROM=GetDeals Admin <noreply@getdeals.co.ke>

# ============================================
# Popular Email Provider Settings
# ============================================

# --- Gmail ---
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=your-gmail@gmail.com
# SMTP_PASS=your-app-password (NOT your regular password!)
# Note: Enable 2-Step Verification and generate App Password

# --- Outlook/Hotmail ---
# SMTP_HOST=smtp-mail.outlook.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=your-email@outlook.com
# SMTP_PASS=your-password

# --- SendGrid ---
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.sendgrid.net
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=apikey
# SMTP_PASS=your-sendgrid-api-key

# --- Mailgun ---
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.mailgun.org
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=postmaster@yourdomain.mailgun.org
# SMTP_PASS=your-mailgun-password

# --- AWS SES ---
# SMTP_HOST=email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=your-ses-smtp-username
# SMTP_PASS=your-ses-smtp-password

# ============================================
# Quick Setup Instructions
# ============================================

# 1. Choose an email provider (Gmail recommended for testing)
# 2. Get your SMTP credentials
# 3. Add the variables to your .env file
# 4. Add the same variables to Vercel:
#    - Go to Vercel Dashboard > Your Project > Settings > Environment Variables
#    - Add each variable individually
#    - Select all environments (Production, Preview, Development)
# 5. Redeploy your application for changes to take effect

# ============================================
# Gmail Setup (Most Common)
# ============================================

# Step 1: Enable 2-Step Verification
# - Go to https://myaccount.google.com/security
# - Click on "2-Step Verification" and follow the setup

# Step 2: Generate App Password
# - Go to https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
# - Select "Mail" and "Other (Custom name)"
# - Name it "GetDeals Admin"
# - Copy the 16-character password
# - Use this as your SMTP_PASS

# Step 3: Update your .env file
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=your-gmail@gmail.com
# SMTP_PASS=abcd efgh ijkl mnop (the 16-char password)
# SMTP_FROM=GetDeals Admin <your-gmail@gmail.com>

# ============================================
# Testing Email Configuration
# ============================================

# After setting up, test by creating a new admin user:
# 1. Go to /admin/users
# 2. Click "Add Admin User"
# 3. Fill in the details
# 4. Submit and check:
#    - Success toast should show "credentials sent"
#    - Check the email inbox
#    - Verify email looks professional
#    - Test login with the credentials

# ============================================
# Troubleshooting
# ============================================

# Problem: "Email not sent: SMTP not configured"
# Solution: Add SMTP environment variables

# Problem: "SMTP verification failed"
# Solution: Check your credentials are correct

# Problem: "Authentication failed"
# Solution: For Gmail, make sure you're using an App Password, not your regular password

# Problem: "Connection timeout"
# Solution: Check your firewall/network settings, try a different port

# Problem: Email goes to spam
# Solution: Configure SPF/DKIM records for your domain, or use a professional email service

# ============================================
# Security Best Practices
# ============================================

# ✅ DO:
# - Use App Passwords (Gmail) or API keys instead of regular passwords
# - Store credentials in environment variables only
# - Use TLS/SSL for connections (port 587 or 465)
# - Rotate passwords regularly
# - Use a dedicated email account for sending

# ❌ DON'T:
# - Commit credentials to Git
# - Share SMTP credentials
# - Use port 25 (insecure)
# - Use your personal email password
# - Store credentials in code files

# ============================================
# Production Recommendations
# ============================================

# For production, consider using:
# 1. SendGrid (99% deliverability, 100 free emails/day)
# 2. AWS SES (cheap, reliable, requires domain verification)
# 3. Mailgun (good API, requires domain verification)
# 4. Postmark (excellent deliverability, transactional emails)

# Avoid using Gmail in production:
# - Daily sending limits (500 emails/day)
# - May be flagged as spam
# - Account can be suspended

# ============================================
# Alternative: Manual Credentials (No Email)
# ============================================

# If you don't configure SMTP, the system will:
# 1. Still create the admin user successfully
# 2. Show a warning toast with the credentials
# 3. Log the credentials to the browser console
# 4. You can manually copy and send them to the user

# This is useful for:
# - Testing/development
# - When email is not available
# - When you prefer manual credential distribution
